```java
import java.text.ParseException;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

public class BimonthlyDateRange {

    public static void printBimonthlyDateRanges(String startDate, int numBimonths) throws ParseException {
        DateTimeFormatter dateFormatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("d-MMM-yyyy");
        LocalDate currentDate = LocalDate.parse(startDate, dateFormatter);

        for (int i = 0; i < numBimonths; i++) {
            LocalDate firstDayOfMonth = currentDate.withDayOfMonth(1);
            int lastDayOfMonth = currentDate.lengthOfMonth();
            LocalDate middleOfMonth = firstDayOfMonth.plusDays(lastDayOfMonth / 2);

            LocalDate endDate;
            if (currentDate.isBefore(middleOfMonth) || currentDate.isEqual(middleOfMonth)) {
                endDate = middleOfMonth;
            } else {
                firstDayOfMonth = middleOfMonth.plusDays(1);
                endDate = firstDayOfMonth.withDayOfMonth(lastDayOfMonth);
            }

            String formattedDateRange = firstDayOfMonth.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("d")) +
                    "-" +
                    endDate.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("d MMM yyyy"));

            System.out.println(formattedDateRange);

            // Move to the next bimonth
            currentDate = currentDate.plusMonths(2);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String startDate = "01-Nov-2023";
        int numBimonths = 6; // You can change this to the number of bimonths you want to print

        try {
            printBimonthlyDateRanges(startDate, numBimonths);
        } catch (ParseException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the code calculates the first day of the month, the middle of the month, and the last day of the month for each iteration. It then checks if the current date falls in the first or second half of the month and calculates the end date accordingly. The result is printed in the desired format.

Your code looks good overall, but there are a couple of things to consider:

1. **Thread Safety:**
   The `SimpleDateFormat` class is not thread-safe. If this code is used in a multithreaded environment, it might lead to unexpected results. It's recommended to either make the `SimpleDateFormat` instances thread-local or use the `java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter`, which is thread-safe.

2. **Consistency:**
   It's a good practice to be consistent in formatting across cases. For example, in the `BIMONTHLY` case, you're using `DateTimeFormatter` for formatting, while in other cases, you're using `SimpleDateFormat`. You might consider using `DateTimeFormatter` consistently for all cases.

