To upload a file to an S3 bucket using a Spring Boot Java program running inside an EC2 instance, you'll need to use the AWS SDK for Java, specifically the Amazon S3 client. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to achieve this:

1. **Set Up AWS Credentials on EC2 Instance:**

   Make sure your EC2 instance has appropriate IAM roles or AWS credentials configured that allow it to access the S3 bucket. This can be done using IAM roles attached to the EC2 instance or by configuring the AWS credentials file (`~/.aws/credentials`).

2. **Create a Spring Boot Project:**

   Set up a Spring Boot project if you haven't already. You can use Spring Initializer or your preferred method.

3. **Add Dependencies:**

   In your `pom.xml` (Maven) or `build.gradle` (Gradle), add the necessary dependencies for AWS SDK and Spring Web:

   ```xml
   <!-- For Maven -->
   <dependencies>
       <!-- ... other dependencies ... -->
       <dependency>
           <groupId>com.amazonaws</groupId>
           <artifactId>aws-java-sdk-s3</artifactId>
           <version>1.11.1013</version> <!-- Use the latest version -->
       </dependency>
       <dependency>
           <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
           <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
       </dependency>
   </dependencies>
   ```

4. **Create the S3 Upload Service:**

   Create a service class that handles the S3 upload logic. For example:

   ```java
   import com.amazonaws.services.s3.AmazonS3;
   import com.amazonaws.services.s3.model.PutObjectRequest;
   import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
   import java.io.File;

   @Service
   public class S3UploadService {

       private final AmazonS3 amazonS3;

       public S3UploadService(AmazonS3 amazonS3) {
           this.amazonS3 = amazonS3;
       }

       public void uploadFileToS3(String bucketName, String key, File file) {
           PutObjectRequest request = new PutObjectRequest(bucketName, key, file);
           amazonS3.putObject(request);
       }
   }
   ```

5. **Create a Controller to Handle File Upload:**

   Create a controller class to handle incoming requests for file upload:

   ```java
   import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
   import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
   import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;
   import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
   import org.springframework.web.multipart.MultipartFile;
   import java.io.IOException;

   @RestController
   public class FileUploadController {

       private final S3UploadService s3UploadService;

       @Autowired
       public FileUploadController(S3UploadService s3UploadService) {
           this.s3UploadService = s3UploadService;
       }

       @PostMapping("/upload")
       public String uploadFile(@RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) throws IOException {
           String bucketName = "your-s3-bucket-name";
           String key = file.getOriginalFilename();
           s3UploadService.uploadFileToS3(bucketName, key, convertMultipartFileToFile(file));
           return "File uploaded successfully!";
       }

       private File convertMultipartFileToFile(MultipartFile file) throws IOException {
           File convFile = new File(file.getOriginalFilename());
           file.transferTo(convFile);
           return convFile;
       }
   }
   ```

6. **Configure AWS Credentials:**

   As your EC2 instance already has the necessary credentials set up, you don't need to explicitly provide credentials in your code. The AWS SDK will use the instance's IAM role or credentials file.

7. **Run the Spring Boot Application:**

   Deploy your Spring Boot application to your EC2 instance, and you can test the file upload by sending a POST request to `http://your-ec2-instance-public-ip:8080/upload` with a `file` parameter containing the file you want to upload.

Remember to replace `"your-s3-bucket-name"` with the actual name of your S3 bucket. This example demonstrates a basic setup; you can further enhance error handling, security, and other aspects based on your requirements.

