Yes, it's possible to pass a function from a TextInput component in React. You can pass a function as a prop to the TextInput component, and then invoke that function from within the TextInput component whenever needed. This allows you to handle various events or actions within the TextInput component and execute the provided function accordingly.

Here's a basic example of how you can pass a function from a parent component to a TextInput component:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

// Parent component
const ParentComponent = () => {
  // Define a function to be passed to the TextInput component
  const handleInputChange = (text) => {
    console.log('Input changed:', text);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Parent Component</h1>
      {/* Pass the handleInputChange function as a prop to the TextInput component */}
      <TextInput onInputChange={handleInputChange} />
    </div>
  );
};

// TextInput component
const TextInput = ({ onInputChange }) => {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');

  // Function to handle input change
  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const text = event.target.value;
    setInputValue(text);

    // Invoke the onInputChange function passed from the parent component
    onInputChange(text);
  };

  return (
    <input
      type="text"
      value={inputValue}
      onChange={handleChange}
      placeholder="Enter text..."
    />
  );
};

export default ParentComponent;
```

In this example, the ParentComponent defines a function `handleInputChange`, which logs the input text whenever it changes. This function is then passed as a prop to the TextInput component as `onInputChange`. Within the TextInput component, the `handleChange` function is called whenever the input value changes, and it invokes the `onInputChange` function, passing the current input text as an argument.

