Instead of using `window.location.pathname`, you should use the `isActive` prop provided by `react-router-dom`'s `NavLink` component to determine if the link is active. This prop takes a function that returns `true` or `false` based on whether the link is currently active. Here's how you can use it:

```jsx
import { NavLink, useLocation } from "react-router-dom";

function App() {
  const location = useLocation();

  const selectedLinkStyle = {
    color: 'white',
    fontSize: '18px',
  };

  const isNavLinkActive = (match, location) => {
    // Customize this logic based on your route structure
    // For example, you can check if the location.pathname starts with the NavLink's "to" path
    return location.pathname === match.url;
  };

  return (
    <>
      {/* ... other code ... */}
      <nav className="vertical-menu">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <NavLink
              to="/tweet-base"
              isActive={isNavLinkActive}
              style={isNavLinkActive(null, location) ? selectedLinkStyle : {}}
            >
              Tweets
            </NavLink>
          </li>
          {/* Repeat for other NavLink items */}
        </ul>
      </nav>
      {/* ... rest of your code ... */}
    </>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this code, we're using `isActive` to define a custom function (`isNavLinkActive`) that checks whether the current location matches the NavLink's "to" path. We also use this function to conditionally apply the `selectedLinkStyle`. This approach should correctly style the active NavLink without requiring a page refresh.

