import React from 'react';

const TaskList = ({ tasks, itemsPerRow = 3 }) => {
  const rows = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tasks.length; i += itemsPerRow) {
    const rowTasks = tasks.slice(i, i + itemsPerRow);
    rows.push(
      <div className="task-row" key={i}>
        {rowTasks.map((task) => (
          <div className="task-item" key={task.id}>
            <strong>ID:</strong> {task.id}<br />
            <strong>Unique ID:</strong> {task.uniqueId}<br />
            <strong>Title:</strong> {task.title}<br />
            <strong>Description:</strong> {task.description}<br />
            <strong>Created Date:</strong> {task.createdDate}<br />
            <strong>Updated Date:</strong> {task.updatedDate}<br />
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    );
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Task List</h2>
      {rows}
    </div>
  );
};

export default TaskList;
```

In this updated code:

- We added a new prop `itemsPerRow` with a default value of `3`. This prop determines how many items will be shown in each row.

- We divide the `tasks` array into rows, each containing up to `itemsPerRow` tasks.

- We map through these rows and render the tasks within each row.

- The CSS classes `task-row` and `task-item` are used to style the rows and individual task items. You can define these styles in your CSS or add inline styles as needed.

