Yes, you can invoke the `checkForDuplicates` method in a static block within the enum. This way, the check will be performed automatically when the enum is loaded. Here's how you can modify the enum to include a static block:

```java
package com.prem.base.exception;

import lombok.Getter;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;

@Getter
public enum CustomErrorCode {

    RESUME_EXISTS(2001001, "Resume exists with unique id %s", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST),
    RESUME_NOT_FOUND(2001001, "Resume not found with unique id %s", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);

    private final int businessErrorCode;
    private final String errorMessage;
    private final HttpStatus httpStatus;

    CustomErrorCode(int businessErrorCode, String errorMessage, HttpStatus httpStatus) {
        this.businessErrorCode = businessErrorCode;
        this.errorMessage = errorMessage;
        this.httpStatus = httpStatus;
    }

    static {
        checkForDuplicates();
    }

    private static void checkForDuplicates() {
        CustomErrorCode[] values = CustomErrorCode.values();

        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < values.length; j++) {
                if (values[i].businessErrorCode == values[j].businessErrorCode) {
                    throw new RuntimeException("Duplicate business error code: " + values[i].businessErrorCode);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Now, the `checkForDuplicates` method will be automatically invoked when the enum class is loaded, and any attempt to create an instance of the enum will trigger the check. If a duplicate business error code is found, it will throw a `RuntimeException`. This approach ensures that the check is performed early during the application startup process.

Yes, in Java, runtime exceptions can occur during the execution of a program. Unlike checked exceptions, which the compiler forces you to handle or declare, runtime exceptions (unchecked exceptions) do not need to be explicitly caught or declared in the method signature. Runtime exceptions typically represent errors that are beyond the control of the programmer, such as attempting to access an array index that does not exist or dividing by zero.

Here's a brief explanation and example:

```java
public class RuntimeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example 1: ArithmeticException (dividing by zero)
        int result = 10 / 0; // This line will throw an ArithmeticException at runtime

        // Example 2: ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
        int[] array = new int[5];
        int value = array[10]; // This line will throw an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at runtime
    }
}
```

In the above example, both `ArithmeticException` and `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` are examples of runtime exceptions. They are not required to be caught or declared, but if not handled, they will result in the termination of the program with an error message.

It's important to note that while unchecked exceptions provide flexibility, it's good practice to handle exceptions when possible, especially in critical parts of your application, to provide better user experience and prevent unexpected terminations. You can use try-catch blocks to handle exceptions or use other mechanisms like global exception handlers depending on the framework or environment you're working in.

