In Handlebars, you can render HTML content by simply including it directly within your `.handlebars` files. Handlebars does not interfere with HTML markup, so you can write HTML tags as you normally would.

Here's an example of how you can render HTML content in a Handlebars template:

```handlebars
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Render HTML in Handlebars</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>{{pageTitle}}</h1>

    <!-- Render HTML content -->
    <div class="content">
        {{{htmlContent}}} <!-- Use triple braces to render unescaped HTML -->
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

In the above example:

- `{{pageTitle}}` is a placeholder for the page title.
- `{{{htmlContent}}}` is where you include the HTML content. Note the use of triple curly braces (`{{{}}}`), which tells Handlebars to render the content without escaping. This is necessary if you want to render HTML tags as HTML rather than treating them as plain text.

When rendering this template with data, you can pass the `pageTitle` and `htmlContent` properties as part of the data object:

```javascript
const handlebars = require('handlebars');
const fs = require('fs');

// Read the Handlebars template file
const template = fs.readFileSync('template.handlebars', 'utf8');

// Compile the template
const compiledTemplate = handlebars.compile(template);

// Data object containing values for rendering
const data = {
    pageTitle: 'Render HTML in Handlebars',
    htmlContent: '<p>This is some <strong>HTML</strong> content.</p>'
};

// Render the template with data
const renderedHtml = compiledTemplate(data);

console.log(renderedHtml);
```

In this example, the `htmlContent` property contains the HTML content that you want to render. When the template is compiled and rendered, Handlebars will include the HTML content as-is in the output.

